/SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>Norwegian University of Life Sciences</Name><Address postal_code="1432"><Department>Faculty of Chemistry, Biotechnology and Food Sciences</Department><Institution>Norwegian University of Life Sciences</Institution><Street>Chr. M. Falsens vei 18</Street><City>Aas</City><Country>Norway</Country></Address><Contact email="matildesterling@gmail.com" sec_email="matilde.kolstad.sterling@nmbu.no"><Address postal_code="1432"><Department>Faculty of Chemistry, Biotechnology and Food Sciences</Department><Institution>Norwegian University of Life Sciences</Institution><Street>Chr. M. Falsens vei 18</Street><City>Aas</City><Country>Norway</Country></Address><Name><First>Matilde</First><Last>Sterling</Last><Middle>Kolstad</Middle></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="BioProject" alias="PRJNA1426923" accession="SRP678656"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP678656</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA1426923</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Shotgun metagenomic sequencing of anoxic and reoxygenated marine sediments from Paddehavet (Norway)</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>Artificial oxygenation can be used to restore oxygen-depleted coastal basins, yet key microbial responses and oxygen-consumption pathways remain poorly understood. The aim of this study was to investigate the initial consumption of oxygen and sulfides in Paddehavet during a reoxygenation experiment. Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed both on samples from a laboratory based oxygenation experiment, on samples from Paddehavet prior to oxygenation and after 4 months of oxygenation. Paddehavet is located in the Oslofjord (Norway). The project was carried out in collaboration with The Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA) and funded by Hafslund.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="22" accession="SRS28234054"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS28234054</P