ity of Life Sciences</Institution><Street>Chr. M. Falsens vei 18</Street><City>Aas</City><Country>Norway</Country></Address><Name><First>Matilde</First><Last>Sterling</Last><Middle>Kolstad</Middle></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="BioProject" alias="PRJNA1426923" accession="SRP678656"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP678656</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA1426923</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Shotgun metagenomic sequencing of anoxic and reoxygenated marine sediments from Paddehavet (Norway)</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>Artificial oxygenation can be used to restore oxygen-depleted coastal basins, yet key microbial responses and oxygen-consumption pathways remain poorly understood. The aim of this study was to investigate the initial consumption of oxygen and sulfides in Paddehavet during a reoxygenation experiment. Shotgun metagenomic sequencing was performed both on samples from a laboratory based oxygenation experiment, on samples from Paddehavet prior to oxygenation and after 4 months of oxygenation. Paddehavet is located in the Oslofjord (Norway). The project was carried out in collaboration with The Norwegian Institute for Water Research (NIVA) and funded by Hafslund.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="35" accession="SRS28234048"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS28234048</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN56219677</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|matildesterling@gmail.com" label="Sample name">35</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>EPC</TITLE><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>256318</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><DESCRIPTION>Positive control</DESCRIPTION><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>1426923</ID><LABEL>PRJNA1426923</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>host</TAG><VALUE>not applicable</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUT